  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nIt is going to sound like a broken record for a while ... but the \nmain concern is and will continue to be prolonged hot / well above \nnormal temperatures across the area. :shrug:\n\nBriefly looking bigger picture, it is a fairly active pattern across \nthe CONUS and Canada as a cutoff low drifts eastward along the \nsouthern Canadian Plains and a leading trough, cutoff low digs down \nalong British Columbia coast and into the PNW, and monsoon activity \nin the SW. It just is not active over our immediate area given the \ninfluence of a sizable area of surface to mid-level high pressure \nremains situated over the Southern Plains and eastward into the \nMississippi and lower Ohio River Valley areas. Pattern evolution \ndoes not favor relief for the area with this ridge gradually \ndrifting eastward through mid-late week before retrograding late \nweek and through the weekend. The retrograde, and amplification, \nwill be in response to cutoff lows dropping into northern New \nEngland and down along the PNW coast respectively, creating an Omega \nBlock look to the pattern. All of this to say, hot and dry remains \nfavored through the going 7-day forecast. \n\nDiving into a bit more detail, there are a few things to note with \nregards to peak heat (and heat index), heat headlines, and any \npotential for even brief relief. To begin though, NBM and LREF \nprobabilities continue to drive home the general prolonged heat \nconfidence. NBM probabilities of MaxT >95 deg F tend to range from \n60 to 90+ percent in areas west of I-35 and south of Highway 36 \nthrough Friday. LREF probabilities of Heat Index >100 deg F are a \nlittle more subdued initially with probabilities widely around 40 \npercent today and Tuesday, but observations from yesterday suggest \nLREF may be a bit conservative. Wednesday through the end of the \nweek LREF probabilities of heat index >100 deg F noticeably increase \nto >50-70 percent across much of the area, in response to signaled \ngradual dew point increases given the prolonged S/SW near-surface \nfetch. Continued forecast overnight lows in the mid to upper 70s \nlikely limit heat relief, especially in the warmer heat \nislands/urban cores. NBM/LREF bolster that confidence as well with \nprobabilities of overnight lows >75 deg F >70-90 percent for much of \nthe area into the weekend. Again, this will be especially true in \nurban areas. A couple of quick caveats or things to watch for. Areas \non the current periphery (or ring of fire as you will) of the mid-\nlevel ridge may see brief relief from showers/convection that are \nable to work far enough south. The most likely time frame for this \nlooks to be Wednesday night into early Thursday. Then late week into \nthe weekend as the ridge retrogrades west and amplifies, there are \nsuggestions that it retreats far enough west that chances for \nshowers/storms could be seen as shortwaves come down the eastern \nside of the ridge. \n\nRegarding the Heat Advisory itself, have extended the headline in \ntime through Friday evening given confidence in prolonged heat as \nnoted above and the potential for late week to be the hottest/most \nuncomfortable with regards to heat index.",
